straightforward, well-tested change. What actually consumed the session was discovering that
craft's automated Homebrew sync had quietly gone stale — `homebrew-tap` had grown branch
protection (a separate, earlier session's own hardening work) that craft's `homebrew-release.yml`
never accounted for. Manually recovering v4.2.0's formula update surfaced 2 more latent bugs in
`homebrew-tap`'s own CI gates (both fixed directly in that repo), then a matching fix was needed on
craft's side too.

**Background-dispatch trust, verified not assumed:** a background session was asked to fix craft's
side of the Homebrew workflow. Its worktree looked stale (4-hour-old last commit, no PR) and its
task notification gave no clear "done" signal — rather than trust either the silence or a guess,
inspected the worktree's actual git log directly, found it had 1 of 2 needed fixes committed but
never pushed, and discovered a *second*, independently-authored PR (#299) already existed solving
the same problem more completely. Closed the duplicate, merged the real one. Same
"verify-the-artifact-not-the-report" discipline as the rest of this session's git/PR work.

**`docs/reports/` established:** `/savant:report`'s own convention (use `docs/reports/` if it
exists, else co-locate with `docs/specs/`) had never actually been exercised — the folder didn't
exist yet, so every prior report landed in `docs/specs/`. Created it, moved the one existing report
in, and added it to the staleness-check exclusion list (same historical/point-in-time rationale as
`docs/specs/`) — a nav-completeness YELLOW on the very next check confirmed the exclusion was
needed, not just following convention blindly.

**v4.0.0 rollback runbook closed out:** generated `docs/reports/REPORT-v4-rollback-runbook-2026-07-16.md`
for the T4.2 spec, then verified against `docs/RUNBOOK-v4-release-rollback.md` (grepped for the
spec's own open question rather than assuming) — confirmed the partial-rollback scenario it
flagged never actually occurred (folio v1.0.0 and craft v4.0.0 both shipped the same day,
2026-07-16, craft now at v4.2.0). Added a one-line historical note to the runbook itself so a
future reader doesn't re-investigate a risk that already passed.

## Session Recap — 2026-07-17 — v4.1.0 SHIPPED: /craft:restore + /craft:refine fix

**Shipped to main:** PR #295 (dev→main merge, tag v4.1.0). Full detail in the `milestone` field
above — summary here is process notes, not a restatement.

**`/craft:restore` build order:** spec-driven-development grill (3 locked decisions: combined
git+`.STATUS` output, no `--sync`, keep default/detailed/summary modes on the git side only) →
`commands/restore.md` thin shim → REFCARD/hub/mkdocs entries → architecture doc. The cross-plugin
`/restore` dispatcher (routing to `savant:restore` for research projects) was the user's original
ask; investigated and descoped after re-confirming craft's `/do`/`/hub` routers structurally can't
dispatch cross-plugin (same finding as the 2026-07-09 native-first-thinning refutation) — surfaced
as an explicit decision via `AskUserQuestion`, not silently dropped.

**`/craft:refine` bug root-caused, not just patched:** the reported "asks for acceptance without
printing first" bug turned out to be a previously-diagnosed-but-never-shipped fix —
`docs/specs/BRAINSTORM-refine-flags-2026-07-01.md` had already designed a fenced-block + 4-way
confirm replacement for the stale Accept/Edit/Use-original vocabulary, but it was never carried
into `skills/workflow/prompt-refiner/SKILL.md` or its 5 callers. Implemented this session, plus an
explicit ordering constraint (both text blocks must render before `AskUserQuestion` fires in the
same turn — a prose skill can't enforce ordering like a hook, so it has to be stated, not implied).

**Release-pipeline friction (branch-guard + tag push):** pushing a git tag while checked out on
`main` gets blocked by `branch-guard.sh` identically to a content push — the fix is the same
documented workaround (checkout `dev` first, tag still applies to `main`'s commit via
`git tag ... main`). Also: a compound `git checkout dev --quiet && git push ...` line gets blocked
*as a whole* before the checkout runs, so the checkout must be its own separate command.

**Homebrew formula, manifest-driven:** bumped `generator/manifest.json` (version/sha256/count) on
a `feature/craft-v4.1.0` branch in `homebrew-tap` (single-integration repo, no `dev`), regenerated
`Formula/craft.rb` via `python3 generator/generate.py` — never hand-edit the `.rb`. Tested by
copying the regenerated formula into the local tap clone (`brew --repository data-wise/tap`) and
running `brew style`/`brew audit --strict`/`brew install` against it, since `brew audit <path>` is
disabled by Homebrew (must reference a tapped name). **Caught a near-miss**: an earlier
`brew install --formula <path>` attempt uninstalled the user's actual `craft` (rejected — "formulae
must be in a tap") without reinstalling anything; recovered immediately via
`brew install data-wise/tap/craft` before proceeding with the correct test method.
PR data-wise/homebrew-tap#153 open, CI green, not yet merged (holds pending explicit merge
approval per the repo's own convention).

**Docs currency check avoided redundant work:** `/craft:docs:update` and a later "fix all issues in
docs" request both surfaced the legacy `utils/docs_detector.py` reporting ~2500 "issues" — spot
verification showed these are overwhelmingly false positives (historical changelog text read as
live staleness, a crude same-directory-implies-related heuristic, one genuine truncation bug in its
own command-name extraction). The authoritative `docs-staleness-check.sh` was already GREEN;
declined to action the noisy tool's output rather than manufacture busywork edits to already-correct
historical text. The truncation bug itself was spun off as a separate background task, already
started by the user in a parallel session — not duplicated here.

## Session Recap — 2026-07-16 — v4 folio split MERGED (#290), gate-divergence closed (#291)

**Shipped to dev:** PR #290 (v4 folio split, merge-commit `4af10f27d`, craft **94→47 commands**),
PR #291 (`ci-bash-suites` validator, squash `88b221da9`), `5034aef7d` (2 stale `docs/commands.md`
count claims). Counts **95/45/8 → 47/40/2**. NOT released — v2.61.2 still the current tag.

**Phase 3.6 closed: 1 router of 5 planned.** `code:audit` shipped (5 cmds→2, the `audit-router`
skill). `orch`, `arch`, `ci`, `plan:feature` all dropped. Every one collapsed on reading the
command bodies in full — which the original grill never did, locking decisions from line counts
and flag names instead. Full ledger: `docs/specs/GRILL-phase-3-6-router-consolidation-2026-07-15.md`
(D11/D12/D13 + the correction log).

**Three CI blockers, three unrelated causes** — the triage is the reusable part:

| Blocker | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `test_branch_guard.sh` red | **Stale branch, not a regression** — a characterization test encoding a known limitation that PRs #287–#289 closed on dev; the suite runs the INSTALLED hook (machine-global state), so it passed on dev and failed on the branch with identical code | **Free with the dev merge — edited nothing** |
| `ci.yml:92` floor 60 vs actual 47 | T3.4, stale a 2nd time (86→60 while the count fell 94→70→47) — floor sat ABOVE the surface | Floor → 40, rationale commented |
| `test_git_shim_correctness.sh` 7 FAIL | **The only true branch debt** — T3.5.2 deleted its subject; a dev merge does NOT fix it | Inverted to guard the migration's end state |

**Root blind spot, now closed (#291):** those are **bash** suites (`ci.yml:177–195`); **pytest does
not run them**. T3.5.2 cited "full pytest 2575 passed" as evidence and shipped the breakage behind
a green check. The new validator derives what to run from the workflows themselves — which
immediately corrected a 4-suite assumption held all session (CI actually runs **6**).

**Evidence discipline that paid off** (worth repeating next session):

- **Planted defects, not green runs.** Both the rewritten shim suite and the new validator were
  proven by deliberately breaking things — including replaying the *real* T3.5.2 bug from
  `d41de18ee`. A test that has never failed proves nothing.
- **Read the CI log, not the badge.** `gh pr checks` reported "all checks done, 0 failing" on #290
  while Craft CI — the only run executing pytest, the bash suites, and the floor gate — was still
  in progress and hadn't registered a check line. Verified via `Found 47 command files` /
  `25/25 passed` in the run log instead.
- **Baseline before blaming.** "7 tests fail" is ambiguous; "21/21 on dev, 7 fail here" localizes
  the defect in one step.
- **A recommendation refuted mid-execution is not a plan to follow.** Folding `issue-check` into
  the git skill was authorized, then contradicted by `tests/test_issue_check_unit.py`'s extraction
  of `classify_issue()` from the command file. Kept it live instead.

**Local env:** `~/.claude/hooks/branch-guard.sh` re-synced (`install-guards.sh`) — #290 merged the
v4 hook text into dev, so the installed hook predated the repo and advised a DELETED command. This
cleared the last known local test failure; the `dev` baseline is now genuinely clean (no more
"1 failed but expected" caveat).

**Open:** Phase 4 (folio `/folio:do` + coordinated release — folio first, craft tag LAST);
`feature/folio-split` worktree + branch preserved for it. `tasks/todo.md` cites a plan-of-record at
`docs/plans/ORCHESTRATE-folio-split.md` that does not exist (the real file is at the branch root).
*(RESOLVED 2026-07-24: `ORCHESTRATE-folio-split.md` moved from repo root to `docs/plans/` as
originally intended, alongside 6 other leftover root-level BRAINSTORM planning artifacts moved to
`docs/specs/` — same session as the docs/website audit.)*
`docs/commands.md:10`'s "17 Categories" needs a definition decision (see milestone). Two
branch-guard false positives observed (`awk 'NR>=203'`, `cp … .bak` — both misread argument text as
a shell redirect creating a code file on dev).

