✅ Resolved 2026-07-01: **Live craft docs-site staleness audit** (`010bbdea`). Ran `docs-staleness-check.sh` (via Homebrew bash 5.3 — macOS's `/bin/bash` 3.2 can't run its `--json` mode, `local -n` nameref unsupported; noted as a real portability bug, not fixed here) + `mkdocs build --strict`. Found and fixed: 7 stale count strings (117→116 cmds, 43→44 skills) across 3 guide/tutorial docs; stale `mkdocs.yml` `site_description` (same drift, missed by the script); 1 real orphan (`docs/reference/GRILL-PROMPT-craft.md` not in nav — added); 2 internal working docs wrongly published (excluded); 1 broken skill-reference link introduced by PR #239's `plan.md` thin-shim (linked outside `docs_dir`, unresolvable — fixed to a plain repo-path reference); 2 pre-existing broken relative links in an archived spec (wrong `../` depth). `docs-staleness-check.sh`: GREEN (was 12 warnings). `mkdocs build --strict`: 0 warnings (was 3). Full suite re-verified: 2072 passed/7 failed/13 errors, exact baseline match.

✅ Resolved 2026-07-01: **Doc-impact scaffolding SPEC synthesized.** [`SPEC-doc-impact-scaffolding-2026-07-01.md`](docs/specs/SPEC-doc-impact-scaffolding-2026-07-01.md) distills the brainstorm+grill into 6 design sections: tiered rubric thresholds (cheap ≥3 / heavy-agent ≥5), the arch-doc double-count subtraction rule, single-scorer propagation to all spec-producers, advisory-but-wired Site-Consistency checklist, and the single-source drift-guard dogfood test. Also closes the missing audit-path e2e test gap (§6): asserts the Site-Consistency checkbox mechanism catches a *real* injected mkdocs-nav drift, not just that the checkbox exists. Not yet implemented — ready for `/craft:orchestrate:plan`.

✅ Resolved 2026-07-01: **orchestrate-dispatch mode — planned.** User gave explicit go-ahead to proceed (no separate ledger re-review needed). Synthesized `docs/specs/SPEC-orchestrate-dispatch-mode-2026-07-01.md` from the grill's 15 resolved branches, then ran plan-orchestrator Mode 1 to generate a 4-phase ORCHESTRATE plan (`dc00475d` in the worktree). Not yet implemented — next session in the worktree starts Phase 1.

✅ Resolved 2026-07-01: **Command-namespace naming rule.** Decided *against* a plugin-wide rename (craft already ~90% `namespace:command`, no depth-3 sprawl; renaming saves ~0 resting tokens per the bloat grill; count-cascade + reference-rot + discoverability cost). Instead adopted the **verb-vs-flag convention** (distinct operation → subcommand; variant → flag) as advisory guidance in `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Rationale: [BRAINSTORM-command-namespace-naming-rule](docs/specs/BRAINSTORM-command-namespace-naming-rule-2026-07-01.md). Optional follow-up (unscheduled): promote the convention to an advisory governance rule (`governance/RULES.yaml` + checker) if enforcement is wanted.

💡 Backlog (cross-plugin, not scheduled): **Teaching-residue audit (craft ↔ scholar).** The 2026-07-01 consolidation audit ([BRAINSTORM-command-skill-consolidation-audit](docs/specs/BRAINSTORM-command-skill-consolidation-audit-2026-07-01.md)) found teaching residue in craft after teaching moved to the `scholar` plugin (teaching-only as of v3.0.0). Two parts: (1) **teaching-specific utils** — `utils/{semester_progress,teach_config,teaching_validation}.py` + `utils/readme-{teach-config,semester-progress}.md` — check whether `scholar` now owns these, then migrate/delete the stale ones; (2) **de-teaching-ify framing** on general commands that kept teaching wording — `git/status.md` ("teaching-specific context") and the `site/*` namespace ("Publish teaching site") — keep the general Quarto/pkgdown/MkDocs capability, drop the teaching-only wording. Higher-value than shim retirement (which the same audit found not worth it — all 56 shims are referenced). **Cross-plugin: must inspect scholar's surface before craft deletes/migrates anything.** Captured 2026-07-01.

